  "body": "Part of the worlds design program: [#5955](https://github.com/developing-today/code/issues/5955). Wave-2 follow-up from the prior-art research (Lane B item 6); parent topic issue: [#5964](https://github.com/developing-today/code/issues/5964). Research source: `workspace/scratch/worlds-capabilities-brief-2026-11-04/prior-art-research.md`.\n\n## Scope\n\nRoc itself offers no runtime module hot-swap (see the shared-library reality-check follow-up under #5955). Compare the three mechanisms that can deliver per-tick user-function switching for #5964, and pick the v1 mechanism:\n\n1. **Wasm target + component-model dynamic instantiation** — components import only declared WIT interfaces (link/instantiation error on mismatch). Caveat from the research: in practice most tooling *statically* links components before shipping; dynamic hot-load/link of arbitrary components remains immature (Bytecode Alliance WASI 0.3-era discussion: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=48504063). Schedule risk is real.\n2. **Process-per-world (or per-function) restart with a warm compile cache** — boring, robust; cost is restart latency, bounded by the compile-cache identity scheme (compile-economics child of #5955).\n3. **Data-driven behavior layer** — user behavior expressed as interpreted data/scripts inside a compiled world (the pattern every interpreted programmable world used: MOO verbs, LSL script replacement).\n\n## Evaluation axes\n\nSwap latency at tick boundary; isolation strength; metering compatibility (per-function gas/timeout research under #5955); determinism for replay; implementation cost against the current host (`pkgs/id/src/world_compile.rs`, `pkgs/id/src/world_session/`).\n\n## Done looks like\n\nA decision doc naming the v1 mechanism, the rejected alternatives with reasons, and what would trigger revisiting the decision.\n",
